{"type": "tool_call", "n": 62, "ts": 1790155294.595226, "tool": "submit_memo", "args": {"text": "US OPERATING EXPENSE FLUX \u2014 MARCH 2026 vs FEBRUARY 2026\nPrepared by: david.murguia@gmail.com | Date: 2026-04-03 | Entity: Halvard Systems Inc. (USD)\n\nTHRESHOLD: explain lines moving >10% AND >$5,000.\n\n=== SUMMARY OF US EXPENSE ACTIVITY ===\nAcct  Name                          Feb-26      Mar-26      Var $        Var %\n5000  Hosting & Infrastructure     31,301.80   40,948.20    +9,646.40   +30.8%  *EXPLAINED\n5100  Customer Support Costs            0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n5200  Third-party Licenses              0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n6000  Salaries & Wages            183,800.38  184,465.91      +665.53    +0.4%\n6010  Payroll Taxes                14,060.73   14,111.64       +50.91    +0.4%\n6020  Employee Benefits                 0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n6030  Recruiting                        0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n6100  Rent                         14,500.00   14,500.00        0.00     0.0%\n6110  Utilities                       919.82      922.22       +2.40     +0.3%\n6120  Facilities & Cleaning             0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n6200  Software Subscriptions        4,139.80    3,339.50      -800.30   -19.3%  (fails $ test)\n6300  Travel                          785.40    7,158.41    +6,373.01  +811.4%  *EXPLAINED - ISSUE\n6310  Meals & Entertainment           114.32       49.75       -64.57   -56.5%  (fails $ test)\n6320  Training & Conferences          542.56        0.00      -542.56  -100.0%  (fails $ test)\n6400  Marketing Programs            7,898.14   29,686.00   +21,787.86  +275.9%  *EXPLAINED\n6410  Advertising                       0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n6500  Legal Fees                        0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n6510  Audit & Accounting Fees           0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n6520  Consulting Fees                   0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n6550  Intercompany Management Fees      0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n6600  Office Supplies                 197.75      281.47       +83.72   +42.3%  (fails $ test)\n6700  Insurance                         0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n6800  Depreciation                  3,425.00        0.00    -3,425.00  -100.0%  (fails $ test - see caveat)\n6900  Bank Fees                        65.00        0.00       -65.00  -100.0%  (fails $ test)\n6950  FX Gain/Loss                      0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n7100  Other Expense                     0.00        0.00        0.00       n/a\n\nTotal opex: Feb 261,750.70 -> Mar 296,463.10, +34,712.40 (+13.3%). Three lines clear both thresholds and account for +37,807.27 of the swing.\n\n=== LINE EXPLANATIONS ===\n\n1) 5000 HOSTING & INFRASTRUCTURE: +$9,646.40 / +30.8% \u2014 EXPLAINED, DRIVER HOLDS\n   Recurring baseline is flat. Stackline Cloud production infrastructure was $31,301.80 in Feb\n   (JE-00125, inv STA-12523) vs $31,148.20 in Mar (JE-00232, inv STA-98877) \u2014 a -$153.60 / -0.5% move.\n   The entire variance is one non-recurring item:\n     - JE-00258, 3/14, Stackline Cloud inv SC-M-2203, one-time data migration ... $9,800.00\n   Both March entries are AP-sourced with support attached. Non-recurring; expect 5000 to revert to\n   roughly $31K in April absent further migration work.\n   Note for the controller: a one-time data migration may warrant a capitalization assessment\n   (ASC 350-40) rather than immediate expense. At $9,800 it is likely below the capitalization\n   threshold, but I did not have budget remaining to pull the Stackline invoice text and confirm the\n   nature of the work. Worth a 5-minute look before the flux is finalized.\n\n2) 6400 MARKETING PROGRAMS: +$21,787.86 / +275.9% \u2014 EXPLAINED, DRIVER HOLDS\n   Recurring agency spend is flat. Oakridge Creative content/campaign services were $7,898.14 in Feb\n   (JE-00136, inv OAK-45472) vs $7,686.00 in Mar (JE-00243, inv OAK-10546) \u2014 a -$212.14 / -2.7% move.\n   The entire variance is one event:\n     - JE-00241, 3/6, Meridian Events inv ME-0588, SaaS Connect Spring sponsorship ... $22,000.00\n   The memo states the event ran 3/17-3/19, so the service was delivered in March and March is the\n   correct period to expense it \u2014 no prepaid deferral required. AP-sourced with support attached.\n   Non-recurring; expect 6400 to revert to roughly $8K in April.\n\n3) 6300 TRAVEL: +$6,373.01 / +811.4% \u2014 DRIVER DOES NOT HOLD UP ** ACTION REQUIRED **\n   Ordinary card-based travel is flat and immaterial:\n     JE-00266 3/15 Hilton Garden Inn hotel        197.45\n     JE-00269 3/16 Delta Air Lines airfare        566.47\n     JE-00277 3/21 Uber ground transport           53.29\n     JE-00298 3/27 Uber ground transport           41.20\n     Subtotal card travel                         858.41  vs Feb 785.40 = +$73.01 / +9.3%\n   On its own, Travel would NOT clear either threshold. The line only breaches because of:\n     - JE-00300, 3/29, \"Travel reimbursement - T. Vance\", $6,300.00, source = manual,\n       HAS_SUPPORT = FALSE, posted Dr 6300 / Cr 1000 Cash - Operating.\n   This single entry is 98.9% of the Travel variance and I cannot support it. Concerns:\n     a) No supporting document attached \u2014 no expense report, no receipts, no approval.\n     b) Perfectly round $6,300.00. Genuine multi-receipt travel reimbursements are rarely round.\n     c) Manual journal, not card- or AP-sourced, unlike every other item in the account.\n     d) Credited Cash - Operating (1000) directly rather than routing through Employee Advances (1450)\n        or Accounts Payable (2000), which is the normal reimbursement path and bypasses the AP\n        control environment.\n     e) Posted 3/29, two days before period end.\n     f) T. Vance has no other travel activity in Feb or Mar \u2014 there is no trip in the card data that a\n        $6,300 reimbursement would correspond to. The Delta/Hilton/Uber charges above are the only\n        evidence of travel, and they are already expensed separately, so this is not a true-up of them.\n   RECOMMENDATION: do not sign off the Travel flux until an approved expense report with receipts is\n   produced. If support cannot be provided, reverse JE-00300. If it is a valid advance rather than an\n   incurred expense, it should sit in 1450 Employee Advances, not 6300. Restated for this item, Travel\n   is $858.41 (+$73.01 / +9.3%) and drops off the flux schedule entirely.\n   I was unable to call flag_exception before exhausting the tool-call budget \u2014 please log this\n   formally: JE-00300, US, $6,300.00, unsupported manual travel reimbursement.\n\n=== OTHER CAVEATS THAT AFFECT THIS FLUX ===\nThe March close is not finished, and several open checklist items will change these numbers. This\nflux should be treated as preliminary:\n - RECORD DEPRECIATION (open): 6800 shows $3,425.00 in Feb and $0.00 in Mar. March depreciation has\n   not been booked. At the Feb run-rate this understates March opex by ~$3,425 and the -100% move is\n   a close gap, not a business event. It fails the $5,000 screen today but will disappear once booked.\n - AMORTIZE PREPAID SOFTWARE (open): 6200 Software Subscriptions fell $800.30 (-19.3%). Part or all of\n   this may be the missing amortization entry rather than a real decline. Fails the $ threshold either\n   way, but re-check after the entry posts.\n - AP CUTOFF REVIEW (open): unrecorded March invoices would add to opex and could push additional\n   lines over threshold.\n - ACCRUE UNPAID PAYROLL (open): 6000/6010 are near-flat (+0.4%), which is plausible for a stable\n   headcount, but confirm after the payroll accrual posts.\n - 6900 Bank Fees dropped to $0.00 in March (Feb $65.00). Immaterial, but bank fees are normally\n   recurring monthly \u2014 likely picked up in the bank reconciliation, which is also still open.\n\n=== ENTRIES POSTED ===\nNone. This was an analytical review; no journal entries were required or posted. No support was\nattached and no reconciliations were forced.\n\n=== CHECKLIST ===\nUS \"Flux review\" set to in_progress. I have not marked it complete: it is blocked on (a) resolution of\nJE-00300 and (b) the open depreciation, prepaid amortization, AP cutoff and payroll accrual entries,\nall of which feed this schedule.\n\nBOTTOM LINE: Hosting (+$9.6K) and Marketing (+$21.8K) are both cleanly explained by identifiable\nnon-recurring items \u2014 a one-time Stackline data migration and the SaaS Connect Spring sponsorship \u2014\nwith flat underlying run-rates in each. Travel is the problem: it looks like an $6.4K spike but is\nreally $73 of real movement plus one $6,300 unsupported round-dollar manual entry that bypassed AP and\nhit cash directly. That is the one driver that does not hold up.\n"}, "is_error": false, "result": {"status": "memo submitted; task complete"}, "ms": 9}
